text:
Raw sequential dependencies
We saw that some reinforcement learning algorithms can find the optimal reward point. They do this by generally contracting the arc if the previous trial was a hit, and expanding it if it was a miss. Do we see this in the data? We know there are some sequential dependencies, although they are of a different kind.

exp1 &lt;- read.csv("output/exp1_data.csv")
dat &lt;- exp1 |&gt; 
  select(subject, experimentorder,blocknumber,trialnumber,setsize,resperr,arc,points) |&gt; 
  group_by(subject, experimentorder, blocknumber) |&gt; 
  mutate(prior_arc = shift_vector(arc, by = 1),
         prior_resperr = shift_vector(resperr, by = 1))

dat |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(prior_arc, arc)) + 
  stat_summary(size = 0.1) +
  geom_abline(slope = 1)







This might be because we are collapsing over participants - even if there is no sequential dependency within participants, the above plot might show one if participants have much different average arcs. We can standardize the arc responses within participants to account for this. For each arc we get its percentile rank such that 0 represents the smallest arc within participant, and 1 represents the highest. We see that there is still sequential dependencies, although a big part of it was accounted for due to between-subject variability.

dat |&gt; 
  group_by(subject, experimentorder) |&gt; 
  mutate(arcp = ecdf(arc)(arc),
         arcp = round(arcp, 2)) |&gt; 
  group_by(subject, experimentorder, blocknumber) |&gt; 
  mutate(prior_arcp = shift_vector(arcp, by = 1)) |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(prior_arcp, arcp)) + 
  stat_summary(size = 0.1) +
  geom_abline(slope = 1)







The lag 1 correlation, computed within participants, has the following distribution:

dat |&gt; 
  group_by(subject, experimentorder) |&gt; 
  summarize(r = cor(arc, prior_arc, use = "pairwise.complete.obs")) |&gt; 
  pluck("r") |&gt; 
  hist(xlab = "Lag 1 correlation", main = "")

Is it regression to the mean?
Klaus wrote:

I wonder how much this effect is due to regression to the mean: Once people settle on an approximately optimal arc width for each experimental condition, random deviations from it towards the narrower end are likely to produce a miss, and deviations to the wider end are likely to produce a hit. The next time the same condition comes around, random deviations towards the narrower end on the last trial of that condition are likely to now be followed by expansions, and vice versa. One way to gauge how much that contributes to the effect could be to shuffle the order of trials.

This is fair, so let’s test it. The potential confound applies not only to the long-range set-size dependency, but also to the original immediate trial dependency.
Check simulated data
First, let’s support that intuition by simulating some data which has to no adaptive arc change but only samples from a distribution. Then we can apply the analysis above to see if we see expansion after misses and contraction after hits as a confound of regression to the mean.
I will simulate data for response errors from the sdm:
\[
y \sim \text{sdm}(c = 4, \kappa = 3.5)
\]
and arcs from a gamma distribution:
\[
a \sim \text{Gamma}(\alpha = 3, \lambda = 2)
\]

library(bmm)
simulate_data &lt;- function(n) {
  data.frame(
    resperr = rsdm(n, c = 4, kappa = 3.5),
    arc = rgamma(n, shape = 3, rate = 2)
  ) |&gt; 
    calculate_expand_likelihood_ratio()
}

replicates &lt;- 1000
trials &lt;- 1000
out &lt;- replicate(replicates, simulate_data(trials), simplify = FALSE)
out &lt;- do.call(rbind, out)


out |&gt; 
  plot_likelihood_ratio()







So Klaus is correct in principle - this simulated data had response error and arc width sampled independently and shows an almost identical effect as with the real data. Thus this finding alone does not indicate real adaptive expansion or contraction in response to hits / misses.
Now that we know this can happen as a byproduct of regression to the mean, let’s see if we can control for it in the real data. To do that, we will do a bootstrap test. We will shuffle the arc widths within participants many times, and find the expected distribution of likelihood ratios under the no-adaptation regression to the mean scenario. Then we can repeat our t-tests against this expected distribution instead of against 1.
Bootstrap test via shuffling trials
First, here’s how the results look after a single shuffling. Almost indistinguishable from the real deal.

exp1 |&gt; 
  group_by(subject, experimentorder) |&gt; 
  mutate(arc = sample(arc)) |&gt; 
  calculate_expand_likelihood_ratio() |&gt; 
  plot_likelihood_ratio()







Here is the bootstrap distribution for the mean likelihood ratios (corresponding to the red points in the plot above. The vertical lines represent the observed mean without the shuffling.

bootstrap_lr_shuffled_arcs &lt;- function(dat) {
  dat |&gt; 
    mutate(arc = sample(arc)) |&gt; 
    calculate_expand_likelihood_ratio() |&gt; 
    group_by(resp_type) |&gt; 
    summarise(mean_ratio = mean(likelihood_ratio_expand))
}
out &lt;- replicate(1000, bootstrap_lr_shuffled_arcs(group_by(exp1, subject, experimentorder)), simplify = FALSE)
out &lt;- do.call(rbind, out)


observed_ratios &lt;- smry1 |&gt; 
  group_by(resp_type) |&gt; 
  summarise(mean_ratio = mean(likelihood_ratio_expand))

out |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(mean_ratio)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 60) +
  facet_wrap(~resp_type, scales = "free") +
  geom_vline(data = observed_ratios, aes(xintercept = mean_ratio), linetype = "dashed", color = "red") +
  ggtitle("Bootstrap distribution of mean likelihood ratios (after shuffling arcs)")







That is strange. In the real data participants contract less after hit and expand less after miss, than what would be predicted from regression to the mean. But the effect is in the opposite direction of what we would expect… If there was real contraction after hit and expansion after miss over and above the regression to the mean, they should have done more of it, not less, than predicted by regression only.
That doesn’t make any sense. Maybe some sort of Simpson’s paradox? Let’s get a separate bootstrap estimate for each subject under shuffling. Long plot coming:

bootstrap_lr_shuffled_arcs_by_subj &lt;- function(dat) {
  dat |&gt; 
    mutate(arc = sample(arc)) |&gt; 
    calculate_expand_likelihood_ratio() |&gt; 
    group_by(resp_type, subject, experimentorder) |&gt; 
    summarise(mean_ratio = mean(likelihood_ratio_expand))
}
out &lt;- replicate(1000, bootstrap_lr_shuffled_arcs_by_subj(group_by(exp1, subject, experimentorder)), simplify = FALSE)
out &lt;- do.call(rbind, out)

out |&gt; 
  mutate(subj_exp = glue::glue("S{subject}_E{experimentorder}")) |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(mean_ratio)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 60) +
  facet_grid(subj_exp~resp_type, scales = "free") +
  geom_vline(data = mutate(smry1, subj_exp = glue::glue("S{subject}_E{experimentorder}")), aes(xintercept = likelihood_ratio_expand), linetype = "dashed", color = "red") +
  ggtitle("Bootstrap distribution of mean likelihood ratios (after shuffling arcs)")







It’s not a Simpson’s paradox. While there are many subject whose likelihood ratios are as expected under random suffling, there is a subset that consistently deviates in the wrong direction (as explained above). I compared this to the overall performance per subject here, and there doesn’t seem to be any obvious relation.
Let’s summarize this by calculating for each subject the percentile of their observed likelihood ratio for expansion relative to the shuffled bootstrap estimates.

boot_percentiles &lt;- out |&gt; 
  left_join(smry1, by = c("resp_type", "subject", "experimentorder")) |&gt; 
  group_by(subject, resp_type, experimentorder) |&gt; 
  summarize(percentile = ecdf(mean_ratio)(unique(likelihood_ratio_expand)))

boot_percentiles |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(resp_type, percentile)) +
  geom_violin(bw = 0.03) +
  geom_point(position = position_jitter(0.1), alpha = 0.4)







We can get a joint score by multiplying the percentile ranks of hit * (1-miss). Values close to 1 means that participants have the weird data where they deviate in the wrong direction from the shuffled expectations. We seem to indeed have two types of participants - about a quarter deviate strongly (percentile rank &gt;= 0.99)

boot_percentiles |&gt; 
  spread(resp_type, percentile) |&gt; 
  mutate(joint = hit * (1-miss)) |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(joint)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 100)








boot_percentiles |&gt; 
  spread(resp_type, percentile) |&gt; 
  ungroup() |&gt; 
  mutate(joint = hit * (1-miss)) |&gt; 
  summarise(weird_prop = mean(joint &gt;= 0.99))

# A tibble: 1 × 1
  weird_prop
       &lt;dbl&gt;
1      0.238

Predicted optimal arc width and hit rates based on the empirical distribution of responses
There are two ways we can check if people’s responses are optimal:


Parametricly. Fit the SDM or another plausible model to the point response distributions, then find the optimum with the same function above based on the inferred SDM distribution

Non-parametricly. Treat the point responses as samples from the underlying distribution and use the empirical cdf to derive the optimum arc width.

In both cases, we are not using the arc-width or phit values at all - only the point responses. Therefore, we can make a completely parameter-free prediction about them.
First, let’s do the non-parametric analysis over different set size conditions. We have 8 set sizes and these are the empirical error distributions, collapsed over subjects:

data &lt;- read.csv("output/exp1_data.csv")
ss_exp &lt;- data |&gt; 
  filter(exp_type == "SetS") |&gt; 
  group_by(subject) |&gt; 
  # the next filter is needed because some participants did two setsize experiments and we only take the first one so that all subjects have the same amount of data
  filter(experimentorder == if_else(length(subject) &gt; 1000, 1, min(experimentorder))) |&gt; 
  ungroup() |&gt; 
  select(subject, blocknumber, setsize, resperr, arc, points, experimentorder) |&gt; 
  mutate(
    hit = as.numeric(points &gt; 0), 
    subject = as.factor(subject),
    setsize = as.factor(setsize))

ggplot(data, aes(resperr)) + 
  geom_histogram(bins = 60) +
  facet_wrap(~setsize, scales = "free")







Next, we find the optimum arc width and phit under these empirical distributions.
First, a sanity check that this empirical approach works. We generate data from the sdm under the c values as above, and now use the generated data to estimate the optimum instead of the theoretical optimum. Then compare with the above theoretical optimum. We have 4000 trials per setsize in the real data, so simulate that many.

find_empirical_optimum &lt;- function(y) {
  Fe &lt;- ecdf(y)
  obj &lt;- function(alpha) (pi - alpha) * (Fe(alpha) - Fe(-alpha))
  fit &lt;- optimise(obj, interval = c(0, pi), maximum = TRUE)
  data.frame(alpha = fit$maximum, phit = 2*Fe(fit$maximum) - 1)
}

n_trials &lt;- 4000
e_optimim_sdm &lt;- lapply(cs, \(c) find_empirical_optimum(rsdm(n_trials, c = c, kappa = 3)))
e_optimim_sdm &lt;- do.call(rbind, e_optimim_sdm)
e_optimim_sdm$c &lt;- cs
e_optimim_sdm |&gt; 
  gather(variable, optimal_value, alpha, phit) |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(c, optimal_value)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.2) +
  geom_line(data = sdm_optim_long, color = "red") +
  facet_wrap(~variable, scales = "free")







It works well - with 4000 observations, the optimums derived from the empirical cdf are clustered closely around the theoretical values. Now, let’s apply this to our real data.

predicted_empirical_optimals &lt;- ss_exp |&gt; 
  group_by(setsize) |&gt; 
  summarise(opt = find_empirical_optimum(resperr)) |&gt; 
  unnest(opt)
predicted_empirical_optimals

# A tibble: 8 × 3
  setsize alpha  phit
  &lt;fct&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1 1       0.489 0.953
2 2       0.698 0.879
3 3       0.733 0.804
4 4       0.908 0.670
5 5       0.960 0.617
6 6       1.01  0.584
7 7       1.17  0.520
8 8       1.24  0.600


Remember, these estimates are based only on the point responses, and do not use any information about the given arc width, or the results hit rate - these are completely separate sources of data. Therefore these are parameter free predictions, which gives us what the optimal arc response would be if the subject knew what the response distribution (or the corresponding memory trace strength distribution) will look like. We can now compare them with the actual values to determine how optimal the behavior is.

observed_arc_phit &lt;- ss_exp |&gt; 
  group_by(setsize) |&gt; 
  summarise(arc_obs = median(arc/2),
            phit_obs = mean(hit))
observed_arc_phit

# A tibble: 8 × 3
  setsize arc_obs phit_obs
  &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
1 1         0.585    0.937
2 2         0.729    0.878
3 3         0.816    0.816
4 4         0.925    0.705
5 5         1.03     0.682
6 6         1.09     0.641
7 7         1.18     0.567
8 8         1.23     0.591



left_join(observed_arc_phit, predicted_empirical_optimals) |&gt; 
  rename(arc_pred = alpha, phit_pred = phit) |&gt; 
  gather(key, value, -setsize) |&gt; 
  separate(key, c("measure", "source")) |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(setsize, value, color = source, group = source)) + 
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(~measure)







The median observed arc, and the hit rates are remarkably close to optimal. From these we can calculate the optimal amount of points, and then get a measure of optimality by dividing the received points by the optimal amount:

points_by_setsize &lt;- ss_exp |&gt; 
  group_by(setsize) |&gt; 
  summarise(points_obs = mean(points))


predicted_empirical_optimals &lt;- predicted_empirical_optimals |&gt; 
  mutate(points_pred = rad2deg(pi - alpha)*phit) |&gt; 
  left_join(points_by_setsize) |&gt; 
  mutate(optimality = points_obs / points_pred)

